  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and created the alert rule, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` covers the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index, with `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, recording:\n- the response code (`response`, stored as a string), bytes sent and the requested URL;\n- the client IP, file extension, user agent and referrer;\n- the client's OS and RAM, and where the request came from and went to (country codes and coordinates).\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-8a6fbb4e Web logs overview\"** has 8 panels:\n- Total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`) and total bytes served, each as a single number\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes per request over time (line chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n- Requests by source country (bar chart)\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-8a6fbb4e-web-logs-overview-0a6ceb05e275\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-8a6fbb4e 5xx spike\"** (id `87979213-4082-4e7d-b80c-0feba5b5d7ab`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field\n- Matches requests with a response code from 500 to 599\n- Runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes\n- It is enabled, and its last status was `ok`.\n\n**Not verified:** I couldn't confirm that the 5xx filter actually matches documents. `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` was empty in my shell, so my test query was rejected as unauthorized. The filter treats the response code as text, which works for three-digit codes. Still, it's worth checking the rule's run history once to make sure it counts the 5xx requests correctly.",
